    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hill chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> who rose as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya</a> feudatories and became an independent kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a> in 1187.</li><li>Their soapstone temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Halebidu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somanathapura">Somanathapura</a>, on star-shaped platforms, are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage</a> masterpieces of sculpture.</li><li>Weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>’s invasions from 1311, the dynasty ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-iii">Veera Ballala III</a> was killed in 1343.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Many Hoysala temple sculptures are signed: carvers such as Mallitamma and Dasoja left their names beside their work, unusually for medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

