    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana.jpg" alt="Vishnuvardhana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishnuvardhana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kannadiga, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hoysalas were hill chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a>, the forested uplands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Dynastic legend, carved on temples as the royal emblem, tells how their ancestor Sala killed a tiger, or a lion, at the command of a Jain teacher at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosevur">Sosevur</a>, modern Angadi, who cried out <em>hoy, Sala</em>, ‘strike, Sala’. The first chiefs known from inscriptions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nripa-kama-ii">Nripa Kama II</a> in the early eleventh century and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapida">Vinayaditya</a>, who ruled c. 1047–1098, were feudatories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> and fought in its wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a>, which then held the old Ganga country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talakad">Talakad</a>. The family rose with the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ereyanga">Ereyanga</a>, a prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> commander, and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-i">Ballala I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>. Vishnuvardhana, who ruled from c. 1108 to 1152, made the Hoysalas a major regional power. In 1116 his generals took Talakad from the Cholas, driving them out of southern Karnataka, and he assumed the title <em>Talakadugonda</em>, ‘conqueror of Talakad’. He moved the court from Sosevur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a>, modern Halebidu, and pressed northwards against his Chalukya overlords, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> contained him. Full independence came only under his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a>, who threw off Chalukya and Kalachuri overlordship and assumed imperial titles in 1187.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra.jpg" alt="Dwarasamudra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Halebidu_monuments_map,_1930_survey,_the_Dorasamudra_of_Hoysala_dynasty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ms Sarah Welch, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hoysala lands joined two economies: the wet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a>, which produced areca nut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, cardamom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and the drier Maidan plateau, where large irrigation tanks built by kings, nobles and merchants watered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>. Many inscriptions record the construction of tanks and the gifts of land to temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> villages that went with them. Trade passed through the west-coast ports, where horses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> were imported for the army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> exported, and merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> were active in towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>. Society was organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and occupation, with landholders, merchant communities and village assemblies taking part in local administration. Religion was notably plural. The early Hoysalas patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>’s queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shantala-devi">Shantala Devi</a> and his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangaraja">Gangaraja</a> endowed Jain shrines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a>. According to tradition Vishnuvardhana himself turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnava</a> philosopher who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melkote">Melkote</a> in the Hoysala country, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> flourished too, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> movement spread among the population in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvaita-vedanta">Dvaita</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>, was born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> on the Hoysala coast in the thirteenth century. Temples served as centres of worship, education, employment and banking.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii.jpg" alt="Veera Ballala II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Profile_of_Amrutesvara_temple_at_Amruthapura_in_Chikkamagaluru_district.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dineshkannambadi, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hoysala army combined infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> with a growing cavalry of imported horses, and much of it was supplied by commanders, called <em>dandanayakas</em>, and by local chiefs. The kings also kept bodyguards known as <em>garudas</em>, sworn to share their lord’s fate; inscriptions record such retainers taking their own lives on the death of their master. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>’s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talakad">Talakad</a> in 1116 broke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> power in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, and his campaigns reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongu-nadu">Kongu</a> region and Nolambavadi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a>, who ruled from 1173 to 1220, fought the Kalachuris and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadavas</a> for the inheritance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> and defeated the Yadava forces at Soratur c. 1190, fixing a frontier along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaprabha-river">Malaprabha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers. In the following generation the Hoysalas became arbiters of Tamil politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-ii-hoysala">Narasimha II</a> intervened repeatedly to protect the weakened Chola king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-chola-iii">Rajaraja III</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadava-dynasty">Kadava</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kopperunchinga-i">Kopperunchinga</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vira-someshwara">Vira Someshvara</a> built a second capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannanur-kuppam">Kannanur Kuppam</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>. These campaigns brought the Hoysalas into conflict with the resurgent Later Pandyan Empire, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-sundara-pandya-i">Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I</a> defeated and killed Someshvara c. 1262. The last major war was against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a> in 1311, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-iii">Veera Ballala III</a> fought on against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>’s governors and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai.jpg" alt="Tiruvannamalai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai">Tiruvannamalai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiruvannamalai_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">my own creation, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hoysala government grew out of a chiefdom into a monarchy modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya</a> court. The king ruled with a council of ministers headed by a chief minister, the <em>mahapradhana</em>, and treasurers, commanders and secretaries, and princes and royal kin often governed provinces. The kingdom was divided into provinces called <em>nadu</em> and <em>vishaya</em>, with smaller units of villages, and local government was in the hands of village headmen, assemblies and the corporations of towns, temples and merchants. Royal revenue came from land, trade and tolls, recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> inscriptions that are among the most numerous of any medieval Indian kingdom. Succession usually passed from father to son, though princes were associated in government during their fathers’ lifetimes. The main kings after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-i-hoysala">Narasimha I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-ii-hoysala">Narasimha II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vira-someshwara">Vira Someshvara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-iii-hoysala">Narasimha III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-iii">Veera Ballala III</a>. In the mid-thirteenth century Vira Someshvara divided his lands between two sons: Narasimha III ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanatha-hoysala">Ramanatha</a> the Tamil territories from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannanur-kuppam">Kannanur Kuppam</a>, an arrangement that led to rivalry and even war between the brothers. The kingdom was reunited under Narasimha III’s son Veera Ballala III, who ruled from 1292 until 1343 and in his last years governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai">Tiruvannamalai</a> in the Tamil country.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hoysaleswara-temple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoysaleswara-temple.jpg" alt="Hoysaleswara Temple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hoysaleswara-temple">Hoysaleswara Temple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoysaleshwara_temple_in_Monsoon.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karthikbs23, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hoysalas are remembered above all for their temples. Built mostly of chloritic schist, a soft soapstone that hardens with exposure, on star-shaped platforms, they carry friezes of elephants, horsemen, scrolls and epic scenes and densely carved images of gods and bracket figures, with many panels signed by sculptors such as Mallitamma and Dasoja. The Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>, consecrated in 1117 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>, the twin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysaleswara-temple">Hoysaleshvara</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Halebidu</a>, begun in the 1120s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennakeshava-temple-somanathapura">Keshava temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somanathapura">Somanathapura</a>, built in 1268 by the commander Somanatha under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-iii-hoysala">Narasimha III</a>, are the finest of the roughly one hundred surviving Hoysala temples. In 2023 they were inscribed together on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage List</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-ensembles-of-the-hoysalas">Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas</a>. The period was also rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature: Harihara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghavanka">Raghavanka</a> pioneered new metres and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janna">Janna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudrabhatta">Rudrabhatta</a> wrote major works on Jain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnava</a> subjects, and Kesiraja’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabdamanidarpana">Shabdamanidarpana</a></em> of 1260 became the classic grammar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-kannada">Old Kannada</a>. The Hoysala court supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scholarship and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavism</a>. Politically, the Hoysala kingdom was the last great independent Kannada state before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>’s invasions, and its commanders and territories formed the base from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> emerged in the 1330s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji.jpg" alt="Alauddin Khalji" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Execution_of_Jalal-al-Din_Firuzshah_II_(r1290-96),_Jami_al-Tawarikh_(Alauddin_Khalji_detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">14th century author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hoysala decline began with the division of the kingdom between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-iii-hoysala">Narasimha III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanatha-hoysala">Ramanatha</a> in the 1250s and the loss of the Tamil territories to the Later Pandyan Empire, whose armies drove the Hoysalas from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannanur-kuppam">Kannanur</a>. The rivalry between the two branches absorbed resources until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-iii">Veera Ballala III</a> reunited the kingdom in the 1290s. Soon afterwards, in 1311, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>’s general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a>, sent south by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a>. Ballala III submitted, surrendered treasure, paid tribute and sent his son to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and he is reported to have guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-misk-kafur">Kafur</a>’s army towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> country. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>’s armies extended Delhi’s rule over the south in the 1320s and Dwarasamudra was attacked again, Ballala III shifted his base to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai">Tiruvannamalai</a> and organised fortified posts along his northern frontier. When Delhi’s grip loosened in the 1330s he took the offensive against the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a>, but in 1343 he was captured near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> and killed; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, reported that his flayed and stuffed body was displayed on the city wall. His son Veera Ballala IV held on briefly, but the remaining Hoysala territories passed by c. 1346 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara-i">Harihara I</a> and his brothers, founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, some of whom had probably served the Hoysalas.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Hoysala Empire grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> hill chiefs into the leading power of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, whose soapstone temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Halebidu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somanathapura">Somanathapura</a> rank among the masterpieces of Indian sculpture, and whose resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> prepared the ground for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>.</em></p>

